---
permalink: /lean-battery/
title: LeanBattery — A battery indicator that doesn't hog your menubar
description: An 11-point-wide macOS menubar battery with per-app energy use, battery health and custom low-battery alerts. Under 0.1% CPU and 0 idle wakeups. Free and open source.
keywords: leanbattery, macos menubar battery, battery percentage menubar, per-app energy, battery health, apple silicon
generate_social_image: false
---

{% extends "./_layouts/base.njk" %}
{% from "components/desktop-frame.njk" import desktopFrame %}

{% block custom_style %}
	{% set css_product %}{% include "css/product.css" %}{% endset %}
	{% set css_lb %}{% include "css/lean-battery.css" %}{% endset %}
	{{ (css_product + css_lb) | cssmin | safe }}
{% endblock %}

{% set repo = "https://github.com/manustays/lean-battery" %}
{% set download = "https://github.com/manustays/lean-battery/releases/latest" %}
{% set brew = "brew install --cask manustays/tools/leanbattery" %}

{# The menubar item itself: the app's own 11 x 22pt glyph. The white-outline
   variants are what macOS draws on a dark menubar; the -light files are the
   dark-outline versions it swaps in on a light one. #}
{% set menubarGlyph %}
<img class="lb-menubar-glyph" src="/assets/img/lean-battery/menubar-discharging.png" width="10" height="20" alt="" />
{% endset %}

{% block main %}
<div class="product">

	{# ---------- Hero ---------- #}
	<section class="block product-hero">
		<div class="product-hero-grid">
			<div class="product-hero-copy">
				<div class="product-hero-head">
					<img class="lb-logo" src="/assets/img/lean-battery/menubar-discharging.png" alt="" width="88" height="176" />
					<div class="product-hero-titles">
						<div class="product-wordmark">LeanBattery</div>
						<div class="product-pronounce mono">11 points wide · the percentage lives inside the icon</div>
					</div>
				</div>

				<h1 class="product-headline">A battery indicator that<br><span class="hl">doesn't hog your menubar.</span></h1>

				<p class="product-lede">A native macOS menubar app that shows battery level and charging state in an 11-point-wide upright battery — with a popover for per-app energy use, battery health and low-battery alerts you set yourself.</p>

				<div class="product-cta">
					<a class="button" href="{{ download }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
						{% svgico 'arrow-circle-down' %}Download for macOS
					</a>
					<a class="button secondary" href="{{ repo }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
						{% svgico 'github' %}View on GitHub
					</a>
				</div>

				<p class="product-note">
					<svg class="product-note-ico" width="15" height="15" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="currentColor" stroke-width="1.8" aria-hidden="true"><circle cx="12" cy="12" r="9"/><line x1="12" y1="11" x2="12" y2="16"/><circle cx="12" cy="8" r="0.6" fill="currentColor" stroke="none"/></svg>
					Apple&nbsp;Silicon MacBooks, macOS&nbsp;26 or later. Builds aren't notarized yet — macOS asks for one confirmation in Privacy &amp; Security on first launch. Fully open source under MIT.
				</p>

				<ul class="product-badges">
					<li>macOS 26+</li>
					<li>Apple Silicon</li>
					<li>Swift 6</li>
					<li>1.3 MB</li>
					<li>MIT</li>
				</ul>
			</div>

			<div class="product-hero-visual">
				{% call desktopFrame(null, transparent=true, app="LeanBattery", statusIcon=menubarGlyph) %}
					<div class="lb-popover" aria-hidden="true">
						<div class="lb-pop-level">
							<span class="lb-pop-pct">78<span>%</span></span>
							<span class="lb-pop-status">On Battery · 5h 40m left</span>
						</div>
						<div class="lb-bar"><i style="width:78%"></i></div>

						<dl class="lb-kv">
							<div><dt>Source</dt><dd>Battery</dd></div>
							<div><dt>Draw</dt><dd>8.4 W</dd></div>
							<div><dt>Temp</dt><dd>31 °C</dd></div>
						</dl>

						<div class="lb-row">
							<span>Low Power Mode</span>
							<span class="lb-switch"></span>
						</div>

						<div class="lb-sec-head">
							<span class="lb-sec-title">Energy</span>
							<div class="lb-ranges"><span class="is-on">Now</span><span>8h</span><span>24h</span><span>7d</span></div>
						</div>
						<ul class="lb-apps">
							{% set demoApps = [
								{ mark: "X", name: "Xcode",   pct: 41, tone: "is-hot"  },
								{ mark: "C", name: "Chrome",  pct: 23, tone: "is-warm" },
								{ mark: "S", name: "Slack",   pct: 16, tone: "is-warm" },
								{ mark: "F", name: "Figma",   pct: 9,  tone: ""        },
								{ mark: "M", name: "Music",   pct: 4,  tone: ""        }
							] %}
							{% for app in demoApps %}
								<li class="lb-app">
									<span class="lb-app-mark">{{ app.mark }}</span>
									<span>
										<span class="lb-app-name">{{ app.name }}</span>
										<span class="lb-app-share {{ app.tone }}"><i style="width:{{ app.pct }}%"></i></span>
									</span>
									<span class="lb-app-pct">{{ app.pct }}%</span>
								</li>
							{% endfor %}
						</ul>

						<div class="lb-row"><span>Battery Information</span><span class="lb-chev">›</span></div>
						<div class="lb-row"><span>Settings</span><span class="lb-chev">›</span></div>
					</div>
				{% endcall %}
				<p class="lb-caption">Illustration with sample data. The narrow glyph in the menubar above is LeanBattery — the wide one beside it is macOS's own.</p>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>

	{# ---------- Positioning callout ---------- #}
	<section class="block product-callout product-highlight-bg product-callout-text">
			<span class="product-callout-dot" aria-hidden="true"></span>
			A battery app that costs you battery is a bad joke — <span class="hl">so this one publishes its own numbers.</span>
	</section>

	<div class="product-highlight-bg">

		{# ---------- The glyph, in every state ---------- #}
		<section class="block">
			<h2 class="product-section-title">One glyph. Every state.</h2>
			<ul class="lb-states">
				{% set glyphStates = [
					{ img: "menubar-discharging",     name: "On battery",   note: "The charge sits inside the glyph, so nothing extra is needed beside it." },
					{ img: "menubar-charging",        name: "Charging",     note: "A plug replaces the number and the fill turns green." },
					{ img: "menubar-low-battery",     name: "Running low",  note: "At 20% or below the fill goes red, before you have to think about it." },
					{ img: "menubar-high-temperature", name: "Running hot", note: "A red dot appears once the battery passes the temperature you set." }
				] %}
				{% for state in glyphStates %}
					<li class="lb-state">
						<span class="lb-chip">
							<img src="/assets/img/lean-battery/{{ state.img }}.png" alt="The LeanBattery menubar glyph, {{ state.name | lower }}." width="88" height="176" loading="lazy" />
						</span>
						<h3>{{ state.name }}</h3>
						<p>{{ state.note }}</p>
					</li>
				{% endfor %}
			</ul>

			<div class="lb-adapt">
				<span class="lb-chip is-light">
					<img src="/assets/img/lean-battery/menubar-discharging-light.png" alt="The same glyph drawn dark, for a light menubar." width="88" height="176" loading="lazy" />
				</span>
				<p>Light menubar? The glyph inverts to match it — the fill stays translucent so the digits read either way.</p>
			</div>
		</section>

		{# ---------- High-value workflows ---------- #}
		<section class="block">
			<h2 class="product-section-title">What you get back</h2>
			<ul class="product-workflows">
				<li class="product-workflow">
					<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'feature-slim' %}</span>
					<h3>Your menubar space</h3>
					<p>An upright battery 11 points wide, with the charge drawn <em>inside</em> it and a plug glyph when you're plugged in. On a notched MacBook that difference is the whole point.</p>
					<ul class="product-workflow-tags">
						<li>11-point icon</li>
						<li>% inside the glyph</li>
						<li>Plug when charging</li>
					</ul>
				</li>
				<li class="product-workflow">
					<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'feature-energy' %}</span>
					<h3>The app that's draining it</h3>
					<p>Top five apps by energy use, with icons and share bars, over the last hour, 8 hours, 24 hours or 7 days — read straight out of the power log macOS already keeps.</p>
					<ul class="product-workflow-tags">
						<li>Per-app energy</li>
						<li>Now → 7 days</li>
						<li>Read-only</li>
					</ul>
				</li>
				<li class="product-workflow">
					<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'feature-bell' %}</span>
					<h3>A warning you actually notice</h3>
					<p>Set up to five of your own thresholds — below 20%, above 80% while charging, whatever you like. Each one can raise a floating pill, pulse the screen edge and play a sound.</p>
					<ul class="product-workflow-tags">
						<li>5 custom rules</li>
						<li>Notch pill</li>
						<li>Screen-edge glow</li>
					</ul>
				</li>
			</ul>
		</section>

		{# ---------- What's in the popover ---------- #}
		<section class="block">
			<h2 class="product-kinds-title">Everything else, one click away.</h2>
			<ul class="product-kinds">
				<li class="product-kind">
					<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'feature-battery' %}</span>
					<h3>Battery header</h3>
					<p>Charge level and whether you're on battery, charging or plugged in but not charging — with time remaining, the adapter's wattage, live system draw and battery temperature.</p>
				</li>
				<li class="product-kind">
					<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'feature-energy' %}</span>
					<h3>Energy by app</h3>
					<p>The top five energy users with their real app icons and share bars, across four time ranges. Rows under 1% are dropped so the list stays readable.</p>
				</li>
				<li class="product-kind">
					<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'feature-health' %}</span>
					<h3>Battery information</h3>
					<p>Health, condition, cycle count, current capacity, voltage and the connected adapter — the numbers you'd otherwise dig out of System Information.</p>
				</li>
				<li class="product-kind">
					<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'feature-lowpower' %}</span>
					<h3>Low Power Mode</h3>
					<p>Flip Low Power Mode from the popover instead of opening System Settings. The icon turns yellow while it's on, so you can tell at a glance.</p>
				</li>
			</ul>
			<p class="lb-soon"><strong>Coming next:</strong> batteries for Bluetooth headphones, mice, keyboards and controllers.</p>
		</section>

		{# ---------- Measured efficiency ---------- #}
		<section class="block">
			<h2 class="product-section-title">Measured, not claimed</h2>
			<p class="product-compare-lede">Numbers from the real app on a real Mac — an M3 Pro MacBook Pro running macOS 27 — sampled once a second by a script in the repo.</p>

			<div class="product-compare-card">
				<div class="product-compare-scroll">
					<table class="product-compare lb-efficiency">
						<thead>
							<tr>
								<th scope="col">What you're doing</th>
								<th scope="col">CPU</th>
								<th scope="col">Idle wakeups</th>
								<th scope="col">Memory</th>
							</tr>
						</thead>
						<tbody>
							{% set efficiencyRows = [
								{ state: "Sitting in the menubar — the 99% case", cpu: "&lt;0.1%",  wakeups: "0 / min",   memory: "~15 MB", edge: true  },
								{ state: "Notification on screen",                 cpu: "0.01%",  wakeups: "0 / min",   memory: "—",      edge: false },
								{ state: "Popover open, <code>Now</code>",         cpu: "0.81%",  wakeups: "—",         memory: "~38 MB", edge: false },
								{ state: "Popover open, <code>7d</code>",          cpu: "0.91%",  wakeups: "1.0 / min", memory: "44 MB",  edge: false }
							] %}
							{% for row in efficiencyRows %}
								<tr{% if row.edge %} class="is-edge"{% endif %}>
									<th scope="row">{{ row.state | safe }}</th>
									<td>{{ row.cpu | safe }}</td>
									<td>{{ row.wakeups | safe }}</td>
									<td>{{ row.memory | safe }}</td>
								</tr>
							{% endfor %}
						</tbody>
					</table>
				</div>
			</div>

			<p class="lb-footnote">
				The menubar figure is three ten-minute runs, measuring 0.06%, 0.08% and 0.06% mean CPU with the popover closed. A one-minute sample usually reads 0.00%, which flatters it — so the honest number is <strong>under 0.1%</strong>, not zero. Reproduce it with <code>scripts/cpu-check.sh</code>.
			</p>

			<p class="lb-footnote">
				<strong>Why it's idle:</strong> there's no polling for battery state. The icon redraws when macOS posts a power-source, Low Power Mode or wake notification, and not otherwise — plus one lax 60-second timer for temperature, the single value macOS has no notification for. The popover's own timers exist only while it's open, and notifications cost nothing at all between events.
			</p>
		</section>

	</div>

	{# ---------- How it works ---------- #}
	<section class="block bg-shaded">
		<h2 class="product-section-title">Running in under a minute</h2>
		<ol class="product-steps">
			<li class="product-step">
				<h3>Install</h3>
				<p>Run <code>{{ brew }}</code>, or download the <code>.zip</code> from Releases and drag <code>LeanBattery.app</code> to <code>/Applications</code>.</p>
			</li>
			<li class="product-step">
				<h3>Approve it once</h3>
				<p>Builds aren't notarized, so the first launch needs System Settings → Privacy &amp; Security → <em>Open Anyway</em>. Right-click → Open no longer works on recent macOS.</p>
			</li>
			<li class="product-step">
				<h3>Forget about it</h3>
				<p>It sits in the menubar. Click for the popover, and turn on Launch at Login in Settings if you want it back after a restart.</p>
			</li>
		</ol>
	</section>

	{# ---------- Open-source trust ---------- #}
	<section class="block product-trust">
		<h2 class="product-section-title">Open source you can trust</h2>
		<ul class="product-trust-grid">
			<li class="product-kind">
				<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'trust-license' %}</span>
				<h3>MIT licensed</h3>
				<p>A permissive license — use, fork and ship it however you like.</p>
			</li>
			<li class="product-kind">
				<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'trust-local' %}</span>
				<h3>Fully local</h3>
				<p>Battery and energy data never leave your Mac. LeanBattery reads macOS's power log; it stores no history of its own.</p>
			</li>
			<li class="product-kind">
				<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'trust-no-account' %}</span>
				<h3>No account</h3>
				<p>No sign-up, no login. Download it and go.</p>
			</li>
			<li class="product-kind">
				<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'trust-no-telemetry' %}</span>
				<h3>No telemetry</h3>
				<p>The only network call it ever makes is a release check — automatically at most once a day, or when you ask. Switch it off and it makes none.</p>
			</li>
			<li class="product-kind">
				<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'trust-code' %}</span>
				<h3>Swift 6, zero dependencies</h3>
				<p>System frameworks only at runtime. Nothing third-party ships inside the app, and 159 tests cover the logic.</p>
			</li>
			<li class="product-kind">
				<span class="ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'trust-lightweight' %}</span>
				<h3>1.3 MB</h3>
				<p>A native AppKit agent app with no Dock icon. No Electron, no bundled browser.</p>
			</li>
		</ul>
		<div class="product-trust-links">
			<a href="{{ repo }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">View source</a>
			<a href="{{ download }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Current release</a>
			<a href="{{ repo }}/issues" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Contributions welcome</a>
		</div>
	</section>

	{# ---------- Support / sponsor ---------- #}
	<section class="block product-support bg-shaded">
			<span class="product-support-ico" aria-hidden="true">{% svgico 'heart' %}</span>
			<h2 class="product-section-title product-support-title">Enjoying LeanBattery?</h2>
			<p class="product-support-text">LeanBattery is free and open source, built in my spare time. If it saves you time, a one-time sponsorship — or even a quick star — genuinely helps and keeps me motivated.</p>
			<div class="product-cta product-support-cta">
				<a class="button sponsor" href="https://github.com/sponsors/manustays?frequency=one-time" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
					{% svgico 'heart' %}Sponsor on GitHub
				</a>
				<a class="button secondary" href="{{ repo }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
					{% svgico 'github' %}Star on GitHub
				</a>
			</div>
			<p class="product-support-note">No pressure — LeanBattery stays free either way.</p>
	</section>

	{# ---------- Closing CTA ---------- #}
	<section class="block product-closing">
		<div class="product-cta">
			<a class="button" href="{{ download }}" target="_blank" rel="noopener">
				{% svgico 'arrow-circle-down' %}Download for macOS
			</a>
		</div>
		<p class="product-license">MIT © 2026 Kumar Abhishek</p>
	</section>

</div>
{% endblock %}
